    "description": "Cognitive Biases That Make You Vulnerable",
    "cards": [
      {
        "id": "12-4-1",
        "topicId": "12-4",
        "topicTitle": "Cognitive Biases That Make You Vulnerable",
        "cardIndex": 1,
        "totalCardsInTopic": 3,
        "title": "Cognitive Biases That Make You Vulnerable",
        "content": "Bro, this is the most important section. **Cognitive biases** are glitches in our brain that lead us to make wrong decisions. Understand them and life becomes easier!\n\n### 12 Cognitive Biases — Detailed Breakdown\n\n#### 1. Anchoring Bias\n\n**Definition:** The first number you see becomes an anchor and all other decisions are affected by it.\n\n**How it affects money:**\nCalculating discount by looking at MRP — \"It was ₹1000, getting it for ₹500, 50% off!\" But did you really need a ₹500 thing?\n\n**Indian Example:**\n\n| Product | MRP | Selling Price | \"Discount\" | Actual Value |\n|---------|-----|---------------|------------|--------------|\n| T-shirt | ₹1,999 | ₹999 | 50% off! | ₹300 worth fabric |\n| Watch | ₹5,000 | ₹2,500 | 50% off! | ₹800 worth mechanism |\n| Headphones | ₹3,000 | ₹1,499 | 50% off! | ₹600 worth sound |\n\nPriya bought a \"₹5,000 bag\" for ₹2,000. She felt she saved a lot. But actually the bag was worth ₹800 — even ₹2,000 was too much!\n\n**How to overcome:**\n- Think: \"Would I buy this without the discount?\"\n- Calculate actual value, not discount\n- Ignore the word \"discount\" — look at price, not percentage\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Loss Aversion\n\n**Definition:** A loss of ₹100 feels 2-2.5x worse than a gain of ₹100 feels good.\n\n**How it affects money:**\n- Losing ₹100 feels 2.5x worse while gaining ₹100 doesn't feel that good\n- That's why people don't make risky investments, even if expected return is high\n- That's why people don't sell stocks at a loss, they keep holding\n\n**Indian Example:**\nAmit invested ₹10,000 in the stock market. Price went to ₹12,000 (₹2,000 profit). Then it went to ₹9,000 (₹1,000 loss). Now he's holding because \"selling will confirm the loss.\" Later it went to ₹6,000 — ₹4,000 real loss!",
        "color": "#10B981",
        "emoji": "🧘",
        "interactiveType": "none"
      },
      {
        "id": "12-4-2",
        "topicId": "12-4",
        "topicTitle": "Cognitive Biases That Make You Vulnerable",
        "cardIndex": 2,
        "totalCardsInTopic": 3,
        "title": "Cognitive Biases That Make You Vulnerable (Part 2)",
        "content": "---\n\n#### 5. Recency Bias\n\n**Definition:** Assuming what happened recently will also happen in the future.\n\n**How it affects money:**\n- Stock market went up last month — thinking it will go up in the future too\n- Rent has been increasing for 3 years — thinking it will always increase\n- Made profit in a scheme for 3 months — assuming it will always profit\n\n**Indian Example:**\nMutual Fund X gave 25% annual return in the last 3 years. Priya thought \"I'm getting 25%!\" and invested ₹2 lakh. Next year market correction came, fund gave -10%. Priya was shocked — \"this wasn't possible!\"\n\n**How to overcome:**\n- Look at historical average, not recent performance\n- Diversify (Module 7 cross-reference)\n- Remember \"Past performance is not indicative of future results\"\n\n---\n\n#### 6. Endowment Effect\n\n**Definition:** You value something more just because you own it.\n\n**How it affects money:**\n- Selling an old phone feels difficult even if you need a new one\n- Selling your shares feels difficult even if they're overvalued\n- Selling an old course feels difficult even if you're not using it\n\n**Indian Example:**\nAmit has an old laptop worth ₹15,000 in the market. He thinks \"it was ₹40,000, I'll give it for ₹25,000.\" But nobody will pay more than ₹15,000. He can't sell it and can't buy a new laptop.\n\n**How to overcome:**\n- Think: \"If I didn't have this, how much would I buy it for?\"\n- Accept market price\n- Separate emotion from reality\n\n---\n\n#### 7. Sunk Cost Fallacy\n\n**Definition:** \"I've already invested so much time and money, I can't quit now.\"\n\n**How it affects money:**\n- Movie is boring but \"I paid for it\" so you keep watching\n- Course isn't useful but \"I spent ₹10,000\" so you keep doing it\n- Stock is at a loss but \"I'll average down\"\n\n**Indian Example:**\n\n| Scenario | Sunk Cost | Smart Decision |\n|----------|-----------|----------------|\n| Boring movie | ₹200 ticket | Leave, save 2 hours |\n| Useless course | ₹10,000 fee | Quit, invest time elsewhere |\n| Losing stock | ₹50,000 invested | Cut loss, invest in index fund |",
        "color": "#10B981",
        "emoji": "🧘",
        "interactiveType": "none"
      },
      {
        "id": "12-4-3",
        "topicId": "12-4",
        "topicTitle": "Cognitive Biases That Make You Vulnerable",
        "cardIndex": 3,
        "totalCardsInTopic": 3,
        "title": "Cognitive Biases That Make You Vulnerable (Part 3)",
        "content": "**Definition:** Dividing money into different categories that doesn't make logical sense.\n\n**How it affects money:**\n- You save from salary but spend the bonus\n- Money is in one account, but you take a loan from another\n- You spend more cash than card\n\n**Indian Example:**\nMeera got ₹20,000 bonus. She thought \"this is extra, I can spend it.\" But actually she had ₹15,000 credit card due. If she had paid the ₹15,000 credit card due (saving 24% interest), it would have been a smarter decision.\n\n**Mental Accounting Mistakes:**\n\n| Category | Mental Account | Actual Impact |\n|----------|---------------|---------------|\n| Salary | \"This is regular, we'll save\" | Often not saved |\n| Bonus | \"This is extra, we'll enjoy\" | Often wasted |\n| Gift money | \"This is a gift, let's spend\" | Often wasted |\n| Cash | \"This is here, it'll get spent\" | Often spent frivolously |\n| Credit | \"This isn't here yet, we'll pay later\" | Debt increases |\n\n**How to overcome:**\n- Treat all money the same — salary or bonus\n- Clear high-interest debt first\n- \"One pot\" thinking — all money is in one place\n\n---\n\n#### 10. Framing Effect\n\n**Definition:** How something is presented changes your decision.\n\n**How it affects money:**\n- \"95% success rate\" vs \"5% failure rate\" — both are the same but the first sounds more attractive\n- \"Free delivery\" vs \"₹50 delivery charge\" — total cost can be the same in both\n\n**Indian Example:**\n\n| Framing | Option A | Option B | Same Thing? |\n|---------|----------|----------|-------------|\n| Insurance | \"₹10 lakh coverage for ₹5,000/year\" | \"₹417/month for peace of mind\" | Yes! |\n| SIP | \"Invest ₹1,000/month\" | \"Save ₹12,000/year for future\" | Yes! |\n| Credit Card | \"3% cashback on all purchases\" | \"Pay 2.5% less on everything\" | Similar! |\n\n**How to overcome:**\n- Look at numbers, not words\n- Calculate total cost yourself\n- Ignore framing, focus on math",
        "color": "#10B981",
        "emoji": "🧘",
        "interactiveType": "none"
      }
    ]
